// @8bitscript/vic20/rasterline — per-line border and background splits, on
// a chip with no interrupt to drive them.
//
// Named by "8bitscript".exports["./rasterline"] in this package's
// package.json, and by "8bitscript".entry.vic20 in @8bitscript/raster's,
// so a portable program's `import { raster, Slot } from
// "@8bitscript/raster"` gets this file on a VIC-20.
// packages/vic20/AGENTS.md, "Raster splits", is the long form of
// everything below and the measurements behind it; read it before
// changing a cycle here.
//
// ---- who applies the list ------------------------------------------------
//
// The VIC raises no interrupt of any kind, so nothing can apply a list "in
// the background" the way the C64's raster IRQ does. What every VIC-20
// program that animates already has is `waitFrame()`, which polls $9004
// for the top of the frame. So the frame runtime does it: FRAME_SYNC.vic20
// names `vic20RasterFrame` as its `frameHook`
// (packages/compiler/src/mos/index.ts), and waitFrame()'s shared routine
// `jsr`s it straight after every frame edge it waits on. The hook
// busy-waits down the frame and writes $900F at each planned line, then
// returns. The portable contract therefore holds unchanged: a list built
// once and `enable()`d shows on every frame, a rebuilt one after
// `commit()`. What it costs, and what a program must know:
//
//   - waitFrame() returns when the LAST entry's line has been written, not
//     at the top of the frame. The program's own per-frame work gets the
//     rest of the frame: an entry at picture line 180 leaves the lines
//     from 230 (NTSC) to the frame's end, about 30 lines, ~2,000 cycles.
//     Put the splits high, or accept that the program runs at a lower
//     rate. There is no way round this on a machine with no interrupt.
//   - A hardware frame waitFrame() does not wait on — the program was busy
//     past the top of the next frame — gets its entries late (every line
//     already passed is written at once) or, if the program misses the
//     frame entirely, not at all: that frame shows the last value written.
//   - On PAL at the default 60 logical frames a second, every sixth
//     waitFrame() returns without waiting (1.2 logical frames per 50 Hz
//     edge), so the program runs two logical frames in one frame's
//     remainder: a PAL program with splits wants frameRate 50, or half
//     that remainder as its budget.
//   - A program that never calls waitFrame() gets no splits.
//   - A program that never imports this file, or imports it and never
//     commits a list, pays nothing: the hook is linked only when reachable
//     code touches the plan it reads (linker/reachability.mjs), and an
//     empty plan returns after one compare.
//
// Interrupts: every VIC-20 program that calls waitFrame() runs with them
// off from start-up (waitframe.ts's rasterSetup `sei`), so nothing can
// steal a cycle from the timed part and this file neither sets nor clears
// the I flag.
//
// ---- what is computed where ----------------------------------------------
//
// Everything that can be worked out before the frame is, in `commit()`:
// each entry's picture line becomes a raster line (+ the vertical origin,
// $9001 × 2 — 50 NTSC, 76 PAL, read then, so a program or KERNAL that
// moved the picture is honoured), then the coarse $9004 value to wait
// for and whether the line synced on is even or odd; entries on one line
// merge into one planned line, since BORDER and BACKGROUND share $900F;
// and both stores' bytes are worked out, each slot the list sets carried
// forward from the entry that last set it (wrapping round the frame, so
// the lines above the first entry show the last one's value, as on the
// C64). Bits the list never sets — a slot it has no entry for, and bit 3
// — are the program's: the hook reads them from $900F once a frame and
// ORs them in. Per planned line it does no arithmetic at all: wait, OR,
// store, wait, OR, store, store.
//
// ---- landing on the line, not near it -------------------------------------
//
// $9004 is raster bits 8-1 and changes every second line; $9003 bit 7 is
// bit 0. Polling $9004 alone lands anywhere in a two-line window. So the
// hook polls $9004 coarsely for the pair before, then waits for $9003
// bit 7 to CHANGE into the line it syncs on: a `bit $9003 / bpl` (or
// `bmi`) loop, 7 cycles, which is the whole jitter left. Odd and even
// lines take different paths to that edge and are padded to the same
// length after it.
//
// Every cycle of a VIC-20 line is on screen somewhere — there is no
// blanking a write can hide in — so a write can only hide behind what
// the slot does NOT show: a BORDER change is invisible while the beam is
// in the picture, a BACKGROUND change while it is in the border. So each
// planned line is two stores: the border half inside the picture of the
// line before, the background half in the border between that line and
// the target. Both land a fixed delay after the edge of the line before
// the target, and the delays are the region's (the VIC-20's line counter
// ticks at a different point of the line on NTSC and PAL, measured —
// packages/vic20/AGENTS.md): the hook has one timed copy per region, and
// `commit()` probes the region once to say which runs. The result: the
// target line is the first whole line in the new colors, on both regions, with nothing visibly moving from frame to
// frame. What it costs is time — a planned line keeps the hook about a
// line and a half — so entries two or more lines apart land exactly and
// an entry on the very next line lands a line or two late.
//
// ---- what a Slot means here ------------------------------------------------
//
// BORDER is $900F bits 0-2 (eight colors, masked & 7) and BACKGROUND bits
// 4-7 (sixteen, & 15); bit 3, normal/inverted video, is never touched.
// CHARSET is $9005 bits 0-3, the character base, with the PET's meaning:
// 0 the upper-case/graphics ROM set at $8000, 1 the mixed-case (text) set
// at $8800 — Video.MEMORY_POINTER_UPPERCASE and _LOWERCASE, the two bytes
// @8bitscript/text already stores, so bits 4-7 (the screen base) are this
// build's own and come from the geometry file, never from a read. Its
// store is the third of a planned line's: in the same border the
// background store hides in, where no character is being fetched. A list
// that sets CHARSET owns $9005 for the whole frame: the hook writes the
// last entry's value at the top of every frame, so the lines above the
// first entry show it however the program left $9005 (text.print selects
// the mixed-case set before every run of text). A print below the last
// planned line still changes the set for the rest of that frame — keep
// the last CHARSET entry at 1 if the program prints there.
// Slot.SCROLL_X is refused: $9000's horizontal origin moves the picture in
// four-pixel steps, not the 0-7 fine scroll the slot means everywhere else.

import { Video } from "./geometry.8bs";

// What the picture does at a line, named without an address. The numbers
// are arbitrary and the same in all nine rasterline files.
export namespace Slot {
    const BORDER: utinyint = 0;     // the border's color, 0-7 here
    const BACKGROUND: utinyint = 1; // the playfield's background color, 0-15
    const SCROLL_X: utinyint = 2;   // the horizontal fine scroll: refused here
    const CHARSET: utinyint = 3;    // 0 upper-case/graphics ($8000), 1 mixed-case text ($8800)
}

// Everything below at a fixed address lives in the cassette buffer
// ($033C-$03FB): RAM no VIC-20 program uses once it is loaded, so the
// list and its plan cost the program image nothing. The hook's asm6502
// block names the plan's addresses as numbers (a block cannot name a
// global: globals are link-renamed), so a change here is a change to
// the hook's operands.
//
// The list as the program built it: three bytes an entry — picture line,
// slot, and the value as its bits in $900F — so `setValue`'s byte offset
// is index * STRIDE, the unit every rasterline file takes.
@address(0x037C)
let entries: array<utinyint, 48>;
// Which planned line each entry was merged into.
@address(0x03AC)
let entryPlan: array<utinyint, 16>;
let entryCount: utinyint = 0;
// True between a change to the list and the commit that hands it over:
// setValue then edits only the list, the way the C64's edits only the
// page the handler is not reading.
let listDirty: bool = false;

// The plan the hook reads.
@address(0x033C)
let planCoarse: array<utinyint, 16>; // wait while $9004 < this
@address(0x034C)
let planEven: array<utinyint, 16>;   // nonzero: sync on the even line of the pair after it
@address(0x035C)
let planFirst: array<utinyint, 16>;  // store 1: the new border, the old background
@address(0x036C)
let planSecond: array<utinyint, 16>; // store 2: both new
@address(0x03BC)
let planCharset: array<utinyint, 16>; // store 3: the whole $9005 byte
// The count, read by the hook in 8BitScript — the global the hook shares
// with commit(), which is what keeps it linked (see the header).
let planCount: utinyint = 0;
// The $900F bits the list does not own: the hook keeps the program's.
let keepMask: utinyint = 0xFF;
// Whether the list sets CHARSET, and the $9005 byte the frame starts with
// when it does: the last entry's, as the colors wrap.
let charsetOwned: bool = false;
let charsetTop: utinyint = 0;
let enabled: bool = false;
// 0 until commit() has probed the region, then 1 NTSC or 2 PAL.
let region: utinyint = 0;

// The frame runtime's hook: FRAME_SYNC.vic20.frameHook names it, and
// waitFrame() calls it after every frame edge. Not for a program to call —
// from anywhere but the top of a frame it waits for lines already passed.
// Ends in an explicit `return;` so the linker keeps it a function
// (mos/index.ts refuses a build where the hook was inlined away).
//
// Cycle by cycle after the edge — the fall-through of `bpl up` (odd) or
// `bmi down` (even): 11 to `store` on either path (odd: lda 4, bne 2,
// jmp 3; even: three nops and a bit $00), then the first padding, then
// 11 to the first store's write (lda zp 3, ora abs,x 4, sta 4), then the
// second padding, then 11 to the second's. So the first store lands
// 22 + P1 cycles after the edge and the second 11 + P2 after that; the
// third, $9005, is a `sty` 4 cycles after the second, its byte loaded
// into Y at the start of the second padding (`ldy abs,x` inside page 3,
// 4 cycles, counted in P2):
//
//   NTSC  P1 36, P2 27 — stores at 58, 96 and 100 cycles
//   PAL   P1 14, P2 25 — stores at 36, 72 and 76 cycles
//
// packages/vic20/AGENTS.md ("Raster splits") has the windows these were
// picked from. There is no taken branch between the edge and either
// store — the padding is a `jsr` into a run of nops (`jsr` + `rts` is 12
// cycles wherever they are, each nop entered 2 more), never a loop — so
// the timing does not change with where the linker happens to place this
// function (a taken branch that crosses a page costs a cycle more). One
// copy per region, chosen once a frame before any waiting starts.
export function vic20RasterFrame(): void {
    let n: utinyint = planCount;
    if (n == 0) {
        return;
    }
    let base: utinyint = memory.read(0x900F) & keepMask;
    // Before any waiting, so none of it is timed. A list without CHARSET
    // entries still makes the third store; it stores $9005's own byte back.
    if (charsetOwned) {
        memory.write(0x9005, charsetTop);
    } else {
        let current: utinyint = memory.read(0x9005);
        for (let p: utinyint = 0; p < n; p++) {
            planCharset[p] = current;
        }
    }
    if (region == 2) {
        asm6502 {
            ; PAL, 71-cycle lines: stores 36, 72 and 76 cycles after the edge
            ldx #0
        pal_coarse:
            lda $9004
            cmp $033c,x
            bcc pal_coarse
        pal_up:
            bit $9003
            bpl pal_up
            lda $034c,x
            bne pal_down
            jmp pal_store
        pal_down:
            bit $9003
            bmi pal_down
            nop
            nop
            nop
            bit $00
        pal_store:
            jsr sled+11
            lda base
            ora $035c,x
            sta $900f
            ldy $03bc,x
            bit $00
            jsr sled+9
            lda base
            ora $036c,x
            sta $900f
            sty $9005
            inx
            cpx n
            bne pal_coarse
        }
    } else {
        asm6502 {
            ; NTSC, 65-cycle lines: stores 58, 96 and 100 cycles after the edge
            ldx #0
        ntsc_coarse:
            lda $9004
            cmp $033c,x
            bcc ntsc_coarse
        ntsc_up:
            bit $9003
            bpl ntsc_up
            lda $034c,x
            bne ntsc_down
            jmp ntsc_store
        ntsc_down:
            bit $9003
            bmi ntsc_down
            nop
            nop
            nop
            bit $00
        ntsc_store:
            jsr sled
            lda base
            ora $035c,x
            sta $900f
            ldy $03bc,x
            bit $00
            jsr sled+8
            lda base
            ora $036c,x
            sta $900f
            sty $9005
            inx
            cpx n
            bne ntsc_coarse
            jmp ntsc_done
            ; the padding: jsr + rts is 12 cycles, each nop entered 2 more
        sled:
            nop
            nop
            nop
            nop
            nop
            nop
            nop
            nop
            nop
            nop
            nop
            nop
            rts
        ntsc_done:
        }
    }
    return;
}

export namespace raster {
    const ENTRIES: utinyint = 16; // the list's capacity
    const STRIDE: utinyint = 3;   // bytes per entry: line, slot, value
    const FINE_SCROLL: bool = false; // Slot.SCROLL_X is refused: $9000 moves the picture in 4-pixel steps
    const COLORS: bool = true;      // Slot.BORDER/BACKGROUND entries are accepted
    const CHARSET: bool = true;    // Slot.CHARSET entries are accepted: $9005's character base
    const FRAME_COUNTER: bool = false; // no video-frame counter: the VIC-20 has no raster or vertical-blank interrupt here
    const LINES: utinyint = 184;  // visible picture lines: Video.ROWS (23) * 8

    // Empty the list. What shows keeps showing until the next commit().
    function clear(): void {
        entryCount = 0;
        listDirty = true;
    }

    // Append: at picture line `line` (0 is the first line of the text
    // area), give `slot` the value `value`. False, and nothing added, if
    // the line is past the picture, the slot is SCROLL_X or unknown, the
    // list is full, or the line is above the last entry's.
    function at(line: utinyint, slot: utinyint, value: utinyint): bool {
        if (line >= raster.LINES || slot == Slot.SCROLL_X || slot > Slot.CHARSET || entryCount >= raster.ENTRIES) {
            return false;
        }
        let offset: utinyint = entryCount * 3;
        if (entryCount > 0 && line < entries[offset - 3]) {
            return false;
        }
        entries[offset] = line;
        entries[offset + 1] = slot;
        entries[offset + 2] = raster.bits(slot, value);
        entryCount = entryCount + 1;
        listDirty = true;
        return true;
    }

    // In order: the same as `at`, at a line above the last entry's — the
    // entries below it slid down a place. False, and nothing added, if the
    // line is past the picture, the slot is refused, or the list is full.
    function insert(line: utinyint, slot: utinyint, value: utinyint): bool {
        if (line >= raster.LINES || slot == Slot.SCROLL_X || slot > Slot.CHARSET || entryCount >= raster.ENTRIES) {
            return false;
        }
        let offset: utinyint = entryCount * 3;
        while (offset > 0 && entries[offset - 3] > line) {
            entries[offset] = entries[offset - 3];
            entries[offset + 1] = entries[offset - 2];
            entries[offset + 2] = entries[offset - 1];
            offset = offset - 3;
        }
        entries[offset] = line;
        entries[offset + 1] = slot;
        entries[offset + 2] = raster.bits(slot, value);
        entryCount = entryCount + 1;
        listDirty = true;
        return true;
    }

    // How many entries the list holds.
    function count(): utinyint {
        return entryCount;
    }

    // Hand the list to the frame runtime: live from the next waitFrame().
    // This is where the plan is worked out, so the hook does nothing but
    // wait and store (the header's "what is computed where").
    function commit(): void {
        raster.probeRegion();
        let top: utinyint = memory.read(0x9001);
        let items: utinyint = 0;
        let lastLine: utinyint = 0;
        let planned: utinyint = 0;
        let owned: utinyint = 0;
        let charset: bool = false;
        plannedEntries = 0;
        for (let i: utinyint = 0; i < entryCount; i++) {
            let offset: utinyint = i * 3;
            let line: utinyint = entries[offset];
            if (items == 0 || line != lastLine) {
                // A planned line keeps the hook about a line and a half,
                // so the next can be no nearer than two lines: one nearer
                // is planned two below the last, where it lands exactly,
                // rather than wherever the hook next catches an edge.
                let target: utinyint = line;
                if (items > 0 && target < planned + 2) {
                    target = planned + 2;
                }
                // Pushed off the picture: this entry and every one after
                // it is not planned (a line the frame may never reach
                // would keep the hook into the next frame).
                if (target >= raster.LINES) {
                    break;
                }
                planned = target;
                // Sync on the line before the target: raster line
                // top * 2 + target - 1, whose pair's predecessor is the
                // $9004 value to wait past — up to 38 * 2 + 213 - 2 on PAL
                // (sixteen entries two lines apart from 183), so 16 bits.
                let before: usmallint = top;
                before = before * 2 + target - 2;
                planCoarse[items] = before >> 1;
                planEven[items] = before & 1;
                items = items + 1;
                lastLine = line;
            }
            entryPlan[i] = items - 1;
            let slot: utinyint = entries[offset + 1];
            if (slot == Slot.BORDER) {
                owned = owned | 0x07;
            } else if (slot == Slot.BACKGROUND) {
                owned = owned | 0xF0;
            } else {
                charset = true;
            }
            plannedEntries = i + 1;
        }
        keepMask = owned ^ 0xFF;
        charsetOwned = charset;
        raster.recolor(items);
        // The hook only runs inside waitFrame(), never during this, so a
        // half-built plan is never read.
        if (enabled) {
            planCount = items;
        } else {
            planCount = 0;
        }
        committed = items;
        listDirty = false;
    }

    // All three stores' bytes for planned lines 0..items-1. Each slot the list
    // sets is carried forward from the entry that last set it, and the
    // lines above the first entry for a slot take the last one's value
    // (the frame wraps, as on the C64). The first store's background half
    // is the line above's: it lands while the old color is still on
    // screen. Whole bytes, never read-modify-write: bits the list does not
    // set are 0 here — bit 3 included, whatever the cassette buffer held —
    // and come from $900F through the hook's base.
    function recolor(items: utinyint): void {
        let border: utinyint = 0;
        let background: utinyint = 0;
        let charset: utinyint = Video.MEMORY_POINTER_UPPERCASE;
        for (let e: utinyint = 0; e < plannedEntries; e++) {
            let at: utinyint = e * 3;
            let slot: utinyint = entries[at + 1];
            if (slot == Slot.BORDER) {
                border = entries[at + 2];
            } else if (slot == Slot.BACKGROUND) {
                background = entries[at + 2];
            } else {
                charset = entries[at + 2];
            }
        }
        charsetTop = charset;
        let entry: utinyint = 0;
        for (let p: utinyint = 0; p < items; p++) {
            let above: utinyint = background;
            while (entry < plannedEntries && entryPlan[entry] == p) {
                let at: utinyint = entry * 3;
                let slot: utinyint = entries[at + 1];
                if (slot == Slot.BORDER) {
                    border = entries[at + 2];
                } else if (slot == Slot.BACKGROUND) {
                    background = entries[at + 2];
                } else {
                    charset = entries[at + 2];
                }
                entry = entry + 1;
            }
            planFirst[p] = border | above;
            planSecond[p] = border | background;
            planCharset[p] = charset;
        }
    }

    // A slot's value as the bits it stores: border $900F 0-2, background
    // $900F 4-7, CHARSET the whole $9005 byte — this build's screen base
    // (a geometry constant, folded) and character base 0 or 2.
    function bits(slot: utinyint, value: utinyint): utinyint {
        if (slot == Slot.BORDER) {
            return value & 7;
        }
        if (slot == Slot.CHARSET) {
            if ((value & 1) == 0) {
                return Video.MEMORY_POINTER_UPPERCASE;
            }
            return Video.MEMORY_POINTER_LOWERCASE;
        }
        return (value & 15) << 4;
    }

    // Once per program: which region, by whether the raster reaches
    // $9004 >= 140, a line only PAL has — the frame runtime's own probe
    // (FRAME_SYNC.vic20.palProbe), over more than a PAL frame. The
    // answer picks the hook's two delays; AGENTS.md has how they were
    // measured.
    function probeRegion(): void {
        if (region != 0) {
            return;
        }
        let pal: utinyint = 0;
        asm6502 {
            ldy #8
            ldx #0
        probe:
            lda $9004
            cmp #140
            bcs found
            dex
            bne probe
            dey
            bne probe
            jmp done
        found:
            lda #1
            sta pal
        done:
        }
        if (pal == 0) {
            region = 1;
        } else {
            region = 2;
        }
    }

    // Rewrite one entry's value, leaving its line and slot: `entry` is the
    // entry's byte offset (index * STRIDE). Live from the next frame with
    // no commit, when the list has not been changed since the last one.
    // False, and nothing written, if the offset is not a multiple of 3 or
    // names no entry.
    function setValue(entry: utinyint, value: utinyint): bool {
        if (entry % 3 != 0 || entry >= entryCount * 3) {
            return false;
        }
        entries[entry + 2] = raster.bits(entries[entry + 1], value);
        if (!listDirty) {
            raster.recolor(committed);
        }
        return true;
    }

    // Start applying: commits the list, which shows from the next frame.
    // No video-frame counter (the VIC-20 has no raster or vertical-blank interrupt here): a count of
    // waitFrame() returns would miss every frame a slow loop skips, so frame()
    // answers 0 and FRAME_COUNTER says so. Count your own waitFrame() calls.
    function frame(): utinyint {
        return 0;
    }

    function enable(): void {
        enabled = true;
        raster.commit();
    }

    // Stop applying; the list is kept. $900F keeps whatever the last
    // write left, as on every machine with a raster list.
    function disable(): void {
        enabled = false;
        planCount = 0;
    }
}

// How many planned lines the last commit() built — setValue's recolor
// walks them while the hook may be reading planCount's copy — and how
// many entries they hold: entries pushed off the picture by the
// two-line rule are not planned, and nothing past this count is read.
let committed: utinyint = 0;
let plannedEntries: utinyint = 0;
